`scripts/contribute` genuinely invokes `gh pr list` / `gh pr create` for
the fork-based external-contributor flow, so a working gh is a dependency
of shipped tooling rather than a convenience. It is currently broken on
this box and fails at the point of use: the distro build (Ubuntu
2.45.0-1ubuntu0.3) emits a malformed User-Agent -- `GitHub CLI ` with a
trailing space, the version string never injected -- which api.github.com
rejects as an HTTP/2 PROTOCOL_ERROR. Every gh API call fails, not just
auth. Surfaced while diagnosing a failed `gh auth login` during the v8.0.0
tag push.

Worth noting why nobody hit this sooner: the core automation (auto-pr,
merge-pr, ci-debug, forgejo-api.sh) talks to api.github.com with curl and
tokens, never through gh, so the daily path was unaffected.
tests/test_contribute_github.py stubs gh, so the suite stays green while
the real binary cannot complete a request -- the logic is proven, the tool
is not.

The allowlist's own rules require a domain plus rationale, and both are
recorded above the entry. The SCOPE LIMIT is stated rather than left
implicit: this covers the apt path only. GitHub serves release-tarball
assets from objects.githubusercontent.com, which is deliberately NOT added
-- broadening a security boundary past what was asked for is how an
allowlist stops meaning anything.

ALLOWED_WEBSITES.md is a governance file (CODEOWNERS: @jgstern). Added
with explicit owner approval in-session, including approval to use auto-pr
without --gov. No consumer parses this file -- the references in
io_boundary.py and stop_hook.py are comments -- so the change cannot move
a verify-claims verdict.

